(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-pathophysiology-i-exam-1-abnormal-cell-growth-quiz"]={"title":"Abnormal Cell Growth and Differentiation Quiz 1 — Clin Path I Exam 1","category":"Clinical Pathophysiology I","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"How do the borders of benign and malignant tumors differ?","choices":["Benign has a ragged border and infiltrates surrounding tissue; malignant is well circumscribed and compresses it","Both have well circumscribed borders, and they are distinguished only by growth rate","Both have ragged borders, and they are distinguished only by whether they metastasize","Benign is well circumscribed and compresses; malignant is ragged and invades"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the benign tumor pushes surrounding tissue aside, the malignant one infiltrates and goes through it. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Which genes promote repair of damaged deoxyribonucleic acid?","choices":["NF-1 and NF-2","Retinoblastoma and adenomatous polyposis coli","p16 and p53","BRCA-1 and BRCA-2"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — a minority of breast cancer patients carry an inherited mutation in one of them. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"Which pairing gives an example of dysplasia with its usual cause?","choices":["Skeletal muscle hypertrophy, usually caused by resistance training","Cervical dysplasia, usually caused by human papillomavirus","Barrett's esophagus, usually caused by chronic acid reflux","Endometrial hyperplasia, usually caused by excess estrogen"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — high-risk human papillomavirus infects cervical epithelial cells, and the resulting uncontrolled proliferation raises the risk of progression from dysplasia to carcinoma. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"What is a metastasis?","choices":["A benign nodule that develops in the tissue draining a cancer","A portion of a cancer that has migrated from the primary site to other sites","A second, unrelated primary cancer arising in another organ","Local invasion of a cancer into the tissue immediately surrounding it"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the primary remains where it began; the metastasis is what has left. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"Which set of features characterizes a benign tumor?","choices":["Autonomous growth, clonal origin, parenchyma and stroma, a single genetic alteration, no stimulus needed","Pleomorphism, abnormal nuclei, mitoses, abnormal differentiation, and invasion","Circumscribed, compresses, often encapsulated, well differentiated, no metastasis, slow","Ragged border, infiltrates surrounding tissue, no capsule, variably differentiated, may metastasize, grows rapidly"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — each has a malignant counterpart: a ragged border, infiltration and invasion, various degrees of differentiation, possible metastasis, and rapid growth. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"What determines where a cancer metastasizes?","choices":["Venous blood flow, tumor and endothelial receptors, and genetic fitness","Chance alone, since circulating tumor cells lodge wherever the vessel first narrows","The size of the primary lesion and the duration of the disease","The histological grade of the tumor and its rate of mitosis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — metastatic spread is NOT random but determined by these three. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"What does eradicating Helicobacter pylori do to cancer risk?","choices":["It has no effect on cancer risk once colonization has occurred","It increases the risk, because the inflammatory response is protective","It reverses established gastric adenocarcinoma","It reduces gastric cancer risk, and some early MALT lymphomas regress"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — eradicating Helicobacter pylori with antibiotics can reduce gastric cancer risk and may induce regression of some early MALT (mucosa-associated lymphoid tissue) lymphomas. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"Which four histological features move a tumor from the benign end towards the malignant end?","choices":["Encapsulation, uniform nuclei, absent mitoses, and normal differentiation","Invasion, lymphatic spread, hematogenous spread, and seeding","Initiation, promotion, progression, and metastasis","Pleomorphism, abnormal nuclei, mitoses, and abnormal differentiation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — pleomorphism, abnormal nuclei, mitoses and abnormal differentiation increase from the benign end toward the malignant end. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"Which statement correctly describes hypertrophy?","choices":["Autonomous proliferation of a clone of cells; especially important in glandular tissue","Enlargement of the individual cells; especially important in permanent tissues such as skeletal and cardiac muscle","An increase in the number of cells; especially important in labile tissues such as epithelium and bone marrow","Replacement of one mature cell type by another; especially important at epithelial junctions"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — it may also occur in other tissues as part of adaptive growth. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"What is a papilloma?","choices":["A malignant tumor arising from supportive, mesenchymal tissue","A malignant epithelial tumor showing squamous cell differentiation","A tumor with finger-like or warty projections from an epithelial surface","A tumor forming a glandular pattern or arising from a gland"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the projections are visible microscopically or macroscopically. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"What happens to proliferation once a cell has committed to differentiation?","choices":["It stays at the same relatively low rate as the stem cell","It becomes autonomous and no longer requires physiologic stimuli","It can be dramatic, but those cells have a limited life-span","It stops entirely, and the differentiated cells become immortal"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the stem cell itself proliferates slowly; the committed progeny do the expansion, and then die. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"Which statement describes familial adenomatosis polyposis syndrome?","choices":["Patients develop a single premalignant polyp in childhood, and colon cancer by age thirty","Patients develop multiple neurofibromas at puberty, and sarcoma by age forty","Patients develop skin cancer in sun-exposed areas from early childhood","Hundreds to thousands of premalignant polyps young, then colon cancer by fifty"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — five hundred to two thousand five hundred adenomatous polyps in the teens and twenties. The responsible gene is adenomatous polyposis coli. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"What distinguishes atrophy from hypoplasia?","choices":["Atrophy is disordered growth of epithelial cells with variation in size and shape","Atrophy is shrinkage of a tissue or organ that had already formed and matured normally","Atrophy is partial development of an organ that never reached mature size","Atrophy is enlargement of an organ because its individual cells enlarge"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the timing is what separates them: hypoplasia is a developmental failure. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"What important caveat attaches to TNM (tumor, nodes, metastases) definitions?","choices":["They are cancer-specific; for some cancers depth of invasion matters more than size","They are identical across every cancer, which is what makes the system universal","They apply only to epithelial malignancies and not to sarcomas","They replace histological grading, which is no longer used"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — which is why a staging table applies to one named cancer rather than all. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"What are the purposes of cancer staging?","choices":["To determine whether the tumor is benign or malignant","Extent of spread, prognosis, and guiding management","To measure how closely the tumor resembles the normal tissue it came from","To identify which gene alterations caused the tumor"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — all three. Staging answers a different question from histological grading. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"Which forms of radiation cause cancer?","choices":["Ultraviolet B, and ionizing radiation","Ultraviolet A only, together with infrared radiation","Microwave and radiofrequency radiation","Ionizing radiation only, with no ultraviolet contribution"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — both ultraviolet B and ionizing radiation cause cancer; unrepaired ultraviolet B mutations explain the skin cancers of xeroderma pigmentosum. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"A benign tumor arises from cartilage. Which category does it fall into?","choices":["Epithelial, since cartilage lines a surface","Glandular, so it would be called an adenoma","Neither, since the classification covers only epithelial tumors","Mesenchymal, since cartilage is supportive tissue"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — mesenchymal tumors arise from supportive tissue: connective tissue, adipose tissue, cartilage, smooth and striated muscle, and bone. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"Why does a tumor mass grow faster than normal tissue?","choices":["A higher proliferative fraction together with a lower rate of cell loss","A higher proliferative fraction together with a higher rate of cell loss","A lower proliferative fraction together with a lower rate of cell loss","An unchanged proliferative fraction with a shorter individual cell cycle"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — both halves matter; it is not only that cells divide faster. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"What is a carcinoma with a glandular growth pattern called?","choices":["A squamous cell carcinoma","A sarcoma","An adenocarcinoma","An adenoma"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — a carcinoma is a malignant epithelial tumor, and one with a glandular growth pattern is an adenocarcinoma. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"Which two malignancies is Helicobacter pylori associated with?","choices":["Cervical carcinoma and oropharyngeal squamous cell carcinoma","Gastric adenocarcinoma and mucosa-associated lymphoid tissue lymphoma","Hepatocellular carcinoma and cholangiocarcinoma","Nasopharyngeal carcinoma and Burkitt lymphoma"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — chronic Helicobacter pylori gastritis is associated with gastric adenocarcinoma and MALT (mucosa-associated lymphoid tissue) lymphoma. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"Which statement correctly identifies the carcinogens produced by the combustion of tobacco?","choices":["Polycyclic aromatic hydrocarbons, causing bladder and lung cancer","Aromatic amines, causing bladder and lung cancer; among the most powerful carcinogens known","Nitrosamines, causing gastric and esophageal cancer","Aflatoxins, causing hepatocellular carcinoma"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — among the most powerful carcinogens known, and tobacco smoke contains numerous others besides. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"In which cavity does seeding most commonly occur?","choices":["The subarachnoid space","The peritoneal cavity","The pericardial cavity","The pleural cavity"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — seeding most commonly occurs in the peritoneal cavity; the pericardial and pleural cavities and the subarachnoid space can also be seeded. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"Which cancer patients may carry an inherited mutation of BRCA-1 or BRCA-2?","choices":["A minority of breast cancer patients","The majority of breast cancer patients","All patients with familial adenomatous polyposis","All patients with xeroderma pigmentosum"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — a minority of breast cancer patients have an inherited mutation of these deoxyribonucleic acid repair genes. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"Which human papillomavirus subtypes most commonly cause cervical cancer?","choices":["Types 16 and 18","Types 6 and 11","Types 1 and 2","Types 31 and 33"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — of many genetic subtypes, these two cause the majority. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"Besides invasion into adjacent structures, which abnormal interaction is a step in invasion?","choices":["Abnormal apoptosis of neighboring stromal cells","Abnormal lymphatic valve function","Abnormal cell-substratum interaction","Abnormal cell-cycle checkpoint control"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the cell's relationship with its own supporting matrix changes. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"Which statement correctly describes tumor suppressor genes?","choices":["They repair damaged deoxyribonucleic acid; the examples are BRCA-1 and BRCA-2","They trigger apoptosis in damaged cells; no specific examples are named","They inhibit cell growth; the examples are NF-1, NF-2, retinoblastoma and adenomatous polyposis coli","They promote regulated cell growth; the examples are growth factors and their receptors"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — losing them removes a brake rather than adding an accelerator. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"What can an adenoma sometimes do that reflects its origin?","choices":["Invade through the basement membrane into surrounding tissue","Seed the peritoneal cavity through an organ surface","Produce finger-like projections from the epithelial surface","Secrete the hormone or hormones produced by its gland of origin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the tumor retains a function of the tissue it came from. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"From how many cells is the proliferating population of a neoplasm derived?","choices":["One cell, which underwent a genetic alteration","Many cells, each of which underwent an independent genetic alteration","Two cells, one parenchymal and one stromal","A variable number, depending on the tissue in which it arises"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the entire proliferating population derives from one cell that underwent a genetic alteration, so the neoplasm is clonal. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"Which two abnormalities separate a tumor cell from a normal cell?","choices":["Abnormal vascular supply, and abnormal stromal composition","Abnormal differentiation, and abnormal cell size","Abnormal regulation of cell growth, and abnormal cell-cell interactions","Abnormal apoptosis, and abnormal deoxyribonucleic acid repair"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — compared with normal cells, tumor cells show abnormal regulation of cell growth and abnormal cell-cell interactions. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"Why do cancers spreading through the portal vein and the inferior vena cava reach the liver and the lungs?","choices":["Because lymphatic drainage from most organs terminates in the liver and lungs","Because those are the organs each vein drains into","Because liver and lung tissue are uniquely receptive to any tumor cell","Because tumor cells preferentially enter arterial rather than venous blood"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — hematogenous spread is typically venous, so fragments in the portal vein reach the liver and those in the inferior vena cava reach the lungs. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 14"}]};
